WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.407
The machines got their
gates in episode five.

2
00:00:04.407 --> 00:00:07.757
This episode is the
human half: members, roles,

3
00:00:07.757 --> 00:00:12.390
and teams — who can build, who
can run, and who gets to see what.

4
00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.755
Here is the workspace roster.

5
00:00:15.755 --> 00:00:20.907
Five people, and next to each name
the role that decides their reach.

6
00:00:20.907 --> 00:00:24.716
This table is the org
chart your AI actually obeys.

7
00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:29.777
Adding someone is a minute of work — name,

8
00:00:29.777 --> 00:00:33.343
email, and the decision
that matters: the role.

9
00:00:33.343 --> 00:00:35.164
Member runs what exists.

10
00:00:35.164 --> 00:00:37.972
Editor builds agents and automations.

11
00:00:37.972 --> 00:00:40.248
Developer wires code and APIs.

12
00:00:40.248 --> 00:00:43.056
Admin holds providers and governance.

13
00:00:43.056 --> 00:00:47.230
We will leave the form here
— the ladder is the lesson.

14
00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:52.376
Notice the shape: most people
never need more than Member.

15
00:00:52.376 --> 00:00:55.438
Roles are not status —
they are blast radius.

16
00:00:55.438 --> 00:00:59.316
The fewer hands on the
machinery, the easier every audit,

17
00:00:59.316 --> 00:01:01.630
every incident, every offboarding.

18
00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:05.081
Teams draw the reading boundaries.

19
00:01:05.081 --> 00:01:10.576
Growth, engineering, customer success —
each can hold its own documents and agents,

20
00:01:10.576 --> 00:01:14.085
and what is scoped to a
team is invisible outside it.

21
00:01:14.085 --> 00:01:19.381
Episode three called it the smallest
library; this is where the walls are built.

22
00:01:20.581 --> 00:01:26.169
And under it all, identity hygiene:
two-factor authentication for every account,

23
00:01:26.169 --> 00:01:30.360
enterprise single sign-on when
your company already has one.

24
00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.551
Boring, decisive, and exactly
where an attacker looks first.

25
00:01:35.751 --> 00:01:40.565
Same principle, both sides of the
table: episode four scoped the agents,

26
00:01:40.565 --> 00:01:42.638
this episode scopes the people.

27
00:01:42.638 --> 00:01:48.253
Access is designed, not assumed — and the
workspace stays explainable because of it.

28
00:01:49.453 --> 00:01:52.664
Members with the smallest sufficient role,

29
00:01:52.664 --> 00:01:56.411
teams as knowledge walls,
identity done properly.

30
00:01:56.411 --> 00:01:59.775
Next episode — the
finale: governance, cost,

31
00:01:59.775 --> 00:02:03.293
and trust, the whole
control room in one tour.

32
00:02:04.493 --> 00:02:09.204
Members, roles, and teams are covered
in depth in the admin section of the

33
00:02:09.204 --> 00:02:10.095
documentation.

34
00:02:10.095 --> 00:02:11.623
See you in episode nine.

